 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S442-002\",\n \"clauses\": {\n  \"count\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 asks the learner to continue a counting sequence. Its seq slot is drawn from F07 and F12 sequences that are derived from sequence, skip_by and direction, so it produces counting problems on the lc's skip values.\"},\n  \"step_2s\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"The skip_by value printed_runs is \\\"2, 5, 10\\\", so the seq slot of F11 can carry a skip of 2.\"},\n  \"step_5s\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"The skip_by value printed_runs is \\\"2, 5, 10\\\", so the seq slot of F11 can carry a skip of 5.\"},\n  \"step_10s\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"What number comes next when counting: {seq}, ___?\", \"reasoning\": \"The skip_by value printed_runs is \\\"2, 5, 10\\\", so the seq slot of F11 can carry a skip of 10.\"},\n  \"up_to_100\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F13\", \"template\": \"(Large group of {a} {names})\", \"reasoning\": \"The slot a repeats the answer value, and its printed_runs reach 100 (\\\"94 to 96, 100\\\"), so F13 prints numbers up to 100. The singular-noun fault in F13 is a separate finding under Rule 1 and does not affect this clause verdict.\"}\n },\n \"failing_families\": [\"F03\", \"F05\", \"F06\", \"F08\", \"F13\", \"F14\", \"F16\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\n  \"Find the rule by comparing neighbours: {bigger} − {smaller} = {diff}.\",\n  \"This {seq_lbl} shows {cnt_lbl} {skip}s ({action} {skip} each time).\",\n  \"{action} {skip} {prep} {last}\",\n  \"{step}: {last} {op}{skip} = {answer}.\",\n  \"(Large group of {a} {names})\",\n  \"(Large group of {a} {pluralize})\",\n  \"How many {names} are there in all?\"\n ],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"Subtraction is in NOT_YET_KNOWN, and the lc (\\\"Count by 2s, 5s and 10s up to 100.\\\") names no subtraction. The declared backward direction reaches the subtraction vocabulary through F05 (action domain members \\\"add\\\" and \\\"subtract\\\"), F06 (\\\"Subtract\\\" and \\\"from\\\" with the minus-style op in F08, whose op members are \\\"+ \\\" and \\\"- \\\"), and F03 (a minus sign between bigger and smaller). F03: the bigger and smaller slots share one members list drawn from the first two terms with no ordering, so bigger can take the smaller term, and the printed statement is then false (for example a member 0 in the bigger slot against 10 in the smaller slot gives a false difference equal to diff). F13 and F14 draw the names slot from _EMOJI_NAMES in singular or plural form under the basis 'a noun from a fixed list, or its plural', and their a slot prints numbers from 24 upward, so a singular such as 'apple' can print after a number like 24. F16 has the same singular fault in 'How many {names} are there in all?'. F15 'Count the {names}.' has no number slot and a singular there is grammatical, so it is not failing. F18 (emoji pictorial) and F20 (number line) are not failing under Rule 1, though F18 includes the emoji \\\"✝️\\\" and \\\"⚔️\\\", which a grade-level reviewer may want to check. Direction backward also feeds F04, F07, F09, F10, F11 and F12 through derived_from_values, but the packet does not show whether backward sequences print in descending order, so those families are not failed on that basis. Clause up_to_100 is produced by F13, which prints a up to 100. Clauses count and the three step clauses are produced by F11, which carries skip_by in its seq.\"\n}",
